          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Life and Later Years</strong><br>\nWhitney married Henrietta Edwards in 1817 and had four children. His later years were spent managing his manufacturing plant in New Haven, Connecticut, which produced firearms and cotton gins.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Views on Slavery</strong><br>\nWhile Whitney's cotton gin indirectly contributed to the expansion of slavery due to increased cotton production, there is little documented evidence about his personal views on the institution of slavery.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nEli Whitney died in 1825, but his legacy as an inventor lives on. He is credited with transforming the agricultural landscape of America and advancing the Industrial Revolution with his innovations in manufacturing.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
